      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Challenges and Improvements</strong><br>\nDespite the system's effectiveness, Braille faced resistance from sighted educators. However, he kept improving the code, and by 1829, he published the first Braille book. He continued refining the system, adding symbols for mathematics and music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Teaching Career</strong><br>\nLouis Braille began teaching at the Institute for Blind Youth in Paris after completing his education there. He was a dedicated teacher who worked tirelessly to improve the lives of the blind. He taught his students not just academics but also life skills and advocated for the rights of blind people.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Adoption</strong><br>\nDuring Louis Braille's lifetime, his system was not widely accepted. It was only after his death in 1852 that Braille started gaining acceptance. Eventually, the system was adopted worldwide and is still in use today.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
