          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Life and Death</strong><br>\nLouis Braille spent his later years teaching and improving his writing system. Sadly, his health deteriorated due to tuberculosis, and he passed away in 1852 at the age of 43. His legacy lived on as his invention became a vital tool for blind individuals globally.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Honors</strong><br>\nAfter his death, Louis Braille's achievements began to be recognized. His house in Coupvray is now a museum, and his name is synonymous with the reading and writing system used by blind people everywhere. Braille's invention opened up new possibilities for literacy and communication in the blind community.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modern Use of Braille</strong><br>\nToday, Braille is used all over the world and has been adapted to almost every known language, including adaptations for scientific and mathematical texts. Technology has further evolved to include Braille displays and readers, ensuring that the blind have access to the ever-growing digital world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
